<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philistines</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–7th centuries BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Philistines were a people who settled the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> in the twelfth century BCE, probably among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, with material culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> origin. They formed a league of five cities, the Philistine Pentapolis of Gaza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a>, Ashdod, Ekron and Gath. Known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> as enemies of Israel, they lost their independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philistines.jpg" alt="Philistines" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ant888nsmb2 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sea_people02_upscayl_1x_remacri-4x.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Philistines lived along the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, in a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> called Philistia. Their identity seems to have begun with the arrival in Canaan of a migrant group from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, thought to have settled around 1175 BCE amid the Late Bronze Age collapse and to have mixed with the indigenous Canaanite societies. Egyptian sources name one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> as the Peleset, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> is said to have settled some of them in southern Canaan after his victory at the Battle of the Delta, around 1175 BCE. Excavation has not confirmed such a settlement.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> says the Philistines came from Caphtor, possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. Most scholars agree on an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> origin, because the figurines, pottery and fire-stands of the Philistine strata resemble Aegean ones. A minority holds that the similarities result from later contact. A study of skeletons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a> in 2019 found that most of their ancestry came from the local Levantine population, with a Southern-European-related share that suggests an influx in the 12th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Philistia was a pentapolis of five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, Gaza, Ashdod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a>, Ekron and Gath, from Wadi Gaza in the south to the Yarqon River in the north. The Philistines are known chiefly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, which tells of their wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>. In it they dominate the Israelites in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samson">Samson</a>, capture the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a> at Aphek and keep it for several months, forbid Israelite blacksmiths so that weapons had to be sharpened by the Philistines, and kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">King Saul</a> and his sons at Mount Gilboa. The same books tell that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goliath">Goliath</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-elah">Valley of Elah</a>.</p><p>The Philistines came under the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> and were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>. In 604 BCE Nebuchadnezzar II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> defeated them, and the sources suggest that his armies destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a> and Ekron after a revolt and exiled much of the population. By the late 5th century BCE the Philistines no longer appear as a distinct group, though how far they were assimilated is debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Excavated Philistine cities show careful town planning with industrial zones. Ekron alone had about 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> installations, and breweries, wineries and shops selling beer and wine are attested, while complex objects of gold, bronze and iron show skilled metalworking. The Philistines worshipped deities already known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>. Their language is poorly attested. Most Philistine names are Semitic, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goliath">Goliath</a>, Achish and Phicol seem non-Semitic, and some scholars suggest that the Philistines first spoke an Indo-European language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philistines” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to this account, the Israelite tribes of the central highlands of Canaan lived without kings in the period of the Judges, but growing pressure from the <b>Philistines</b> of the coastal plain, who captured the Ark of the Covenant, led them to demand a king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Philistines</b>, who settled in the southern Levant in the 12th century BCE, at first made pottery derived from late Mycenaean styles, which suggests that some of them came from the Aegean world.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>…a Semitic name and the story is set &quot;in the days of Shamgar&quot;, a hero known for fighting the <b>Philistines</b>, so some, among them Łukasz Niesiołowski-Spanò, believe it is really about the Sea Peoples; a layer of Hazor from about 1200 BCE shows signs of a catastrophic fire.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-deborah">Song of Deborah</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1175 BCE Ramesses III of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom repelled the Sea Peoples, including the <b>Peleset</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/philistine-pentapolis">Philistine Pentapolis</a></li></ul></section>
